Issue:
Placeholder text in inputs inherits the browser default styling, which renders in a light grey and, in Firefox, at reduced opacity. Against typical white field backgrounds this regularly falls below the 4.5:1 contrast ratio.

Low vision users can't read the hint text, and because placeholder text is often the only example of the expected format, the field's purpose becomes harder to work out. This fails Success Criterion 1.4.3 (Contrast (Minimum)).

*Recommendation*

Set placeholder color to the field's text color (or another value with at least 4.5:1 contrast) and reset Firefox's default opacity.

*After*

{code}::placeholder {

color: currentColor;

opacity: 1;

}{code}

*MagicBullet:* Currently patched sitewide by {{init.css}} ({{::-moz-placeholder}} and {{::-webkit-input-placeholder}} rules).

The base theme and normalize CSS remove the default focus outline from {{button}} elements without a replacement indicator in Firefox.

Keyboard users in Firefox can't see which button currently has focus, so they lose their place when tabbing through forms, filters, and dialogs. This fails Success Criterion 2.4.7 (Focus Visible).

*Recommendation*

1. Remove the outline reset from the base stylesheet, or replace it with a clearly visible {{:focus-visible}} style for all buttons.
2. Verify focus is visible on every button in Firefox, Chrome, and Safari.

*After*

{code}button:focus-visible {

outline: 2px solid currentColor;

outline-offset: 2px;

}{code}

*More Info*

* [https://github.com/radancy-pe/uid-base-gst/issues/155|https://github.com/radancy-pe/uid-base-gst/issues/155]

*MagicBullet:* Currently patched sitewide by {{init.css}}, which forces {{outline-style: auto !important}} on {{button:-moz-focusring}} as a stopgap.

The {{a.access-link}} skip link appears on focus, but its theme styling removes or weakens the focus outline and places it behind sticky headers, cookie banners, and other high {{z-index}} content.

Keyboard users who press Tab first may not see the skip link at all, or can't tell it's focused, so they don't know the bypass mechanism exists. This fails Success Criterion 2.4.7 (Focus Visible) and 2.4.11 (Focus Not Obscured (Minimum)), and undermines 2.4.1 (Bypass Blocks).

*Recommendation*

1. Restore the browser's default focus outline (or a clearly visible custom one) on the skip link, offset so it isn't clipped.
2. Position it in the top-left corner on focus and give it a stacking order above all sticky and fixed content.

*After*

{code}a.access-link {

outline-color: revert;

outline-offset: 4px;

top: 8px;

z-index: 2147483647;

}{code}

*MagicBullet:* Currently patched sitewide by {{init.css}} ({{a.access-link}} rules).

When arrowing through MindReader suggestions, the {{.active}} suggestion often has no visible highlight, and the links are inline so any background only covers the text.

Keyboard users, including sighted screen reader and low vision users, can't see which suggestion Enter will select. This fails Success Criterion 2.4.7 (Focus Visible) and 1.4.11 (Non-text Contrast).

*Recommendation*

Make suggestion links block-level with padding and give the active item a visible background change plus an outline with sufficient contrast.

*After*

{code}.mindreader-results a {

display: block;

padding: .5em 1em;

}

.mindreader-results a.active {

background-color: #eee;

outline: 1px #000 solid;

outline-offset: -4px;

}{code}

*MagicBullet:* Currently patched sitewide by {{init.css}} ({{.mindreader-results}} rules).

The Advanced Job Description header ({{#ajd-header}}) stays sticky regardless of viewport height. At short heights, such as landscape phones or desktop browsers zoomed to 200% or more, it takes up a large share of the screen.

Focused elements scroll underneath it and become hidden, and the remaining reading area is too small to use. This fails Success Criterion 2.4.11 (Focus Not Obscured (Minimum)), 1.4.10 (Reflow), and 1.4.4 (Resize Text).

*Recommendation*

Disable sticky positioning when the viewport is short.

*After*

{code}@media (height <= 600px) {

#ajd-header { position: relative; }

}{code}

*MagicBullet:* Currently patched sitewide by {{init.css}}.

Data form fields that collect personal information (first name, last name, email, phone, street address, city, state/province, postal code) have no {{autocomplete}} tokens, and the email field uses {{type="text"}}.

Users with motor or cognitive disabilities can't have the browser fill in these fields, and mobile users don't get the email keyboard. This fails Success Criterion 1.3.5 (Identify Input Purpose).

*Recommendation*

Add these {{autocomplete}} tokens and change the email field to {{type="email"}}:

* {{FirstName}}: {{given-name}}
* {{LastName}}: {{family-name}}
* {{EmailAddress}}: {{email}}
* {{MobilePhone}}: {{tel}}
* {{StreetAddress}}: {{street-address}}
* {{City}}: {{address-level2}}
* State/Province: {{address-level1}}
* {{PostalCode}}: {{postal-code}}

*Before*

{code}<input name="EmailAddress" type="text">{code}

*After*

{code}<input name="EmailAddress" type="email" autocomplete="email">{code}

*MagicBullet:* Currently patched sitewide by {{fixDataForm()}}. State/Province is currently targeted by a client-specific field name ({{Custom-92392}}), so it needs a product-side solution. Documented as [A11Y004|https://radancy.dev/magicbullet/a11y/#A11Y004].
